# Milestone: v0.9.4
# Covers: nested `every (P1) { every (P2) X }` — outer periodic whose body
#         installs an inner periodic each fire.  PINS current v0.9.4 broken
#         behaviour: the inner every() install fails with
#         `CLOSURE: proto index out of range` from src/vm/uvm.c OP_CLOSURE.
#         Filed as a v1.x activation target.
#
# Root cause (provisional): OP_CLOSURE looks up child_proto via
#     executing_proto = cur_cl->proto (or s->root_proto at chunk-top)
#     child_proto    = executing_proto->nested[bx]
# An every() body strand executes as a synthesized closure whose proto's
# nested[] is empty / doesn't contain the inner every()'s body proto.
# The inner every() compiles to an OP_CLOSURE+install pair where the
# CLOSURE op then can't find its child proto in the body strand's
# executing_proto.nested[].  Manifests for ANY OP_CLOSURE-emitting
# construct inside an every() body, not just nested every():
#     every (P) { var g = function() { 1 } }       # same error
#     every (P) { every (Q) X }                    # same error
# See controller dialog 2026-05-20 Task 5.5 — confirmed via REPL repro
# `printf '... every (10us) { every (1us) ... }' | urbi -i`.
#
# Reduced scope rationale: per the task brief ("just verify nesting
# compiles, runs, and the body fires at least once"), the runtime-fire
# path is blocked by this bug.  What IS true and worth pinning:
#   - The source parses (no compile error).
#   - The outer every() installs and fires (no install-time error).
#   - The inner every() install raises TypeError per OP_CLOSURE check.
# This fixture captures that exact shape so any regression flips a
# diff: either the parse breaks, the outer install breaks, or v1.x
# fixes the inner-CLOSURE bug (in which case rewrite this fixture
# to assert `Realm.inner_count > 0` per the original intent).
#
# Open semantic questions deferred to v1.x activation (once the
# OP_CLOSURE-in-every-body bug is fixed):
#   - Outer-fire accumulation: each outer fire spawns a FRESH inner
#     UPeriodic.  Legacy urbi 2.x presumably scopes the inner periodic
#     to the outer body-strand's tag chain so completion unhooks it.
#     Confirm.
#   - Tag-chain inheritance: when an inner every() is installed from
#     inside an outer every() body strand, does the inner inherit the
#     outer's user-tag (if any)?  Spec #3 §3.1 — innermost_user_tag at
#     install time.  Confirm.

var inner_count = 0
every (10us) { every (1us) Realm.inner_count = Realm.inner_count + 1 }
0
0
Realm.inner_count > 0
[00000000] 0
[00000000] nil
[00000000] !!! TypeError: CLOSURE: proto index out of range
[00000000] !!! TypeError: CLOSURE: proto index out of range
[00000000] !!! TypeError: CLOSURE: proto index out of range
